{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_028",
  "condition": "paraphrase",
  "rewritten_text": "I have already enjoyed such success in two petitions for pardon that I risk becoming overly confident about yours, and about those of your acquaintances who might have any cause for grievance. You must all strive to grasp the precise circumstances of my arrival at Randalls; you must view me as a man bearing a secret that had to be guarded at any cost. That was the fact. Whether I was entitled to place myself in a position demanding such concealment is another matter, which I shall not treat here. For my inclination to deem it a right, I point every doubter toward a brick house with sash windows below and casements above in Highbury. I dared not address her openly; my difficulties in the then state of Enscombe were too well known to need definition, and I was fortunate enough to succeed before we separated at Weymouth, persuading the most upright female mind in creation to bend in charity toward a secret engagement. Had she declined, I should have gone mad. Yet you will be ready to ask, what was my hope in doing this? What did I anticipate? Anything and everything—time, chance, circumstance, gradual influences, sudden eruptions, perseverance and fatigue, health and illness. Every prospect of good lay before me, and the first blessing was secured when she promised fidelity and correspondence. If further explanation is required, I have the honor, dear madam, of being your husband’s son, and the benefit of inheriting a disposition to hope for good that no inheritance of houses or lands can ever match. Imagine me, then, under these conditions, arriving on my first visit to Randalls; and I am aware of a fault, for that visit might have been paid for sooner. You will look back and see that I did not come until Miss Fairfax was in Highbury; and as you were the one slighted, you will forgive me at once; but I must appeal to my father’s compassion, reminding him that as long as I was away from his house, I lost the blessing of knowing you. My conduct during the very happy fortnight I spent with you, I hope, did not expose me to reproach, except on one point. Now I come to the principal, the only significant part of my behaviour while attached to you, which causes me anxiety or demands careful explanation. With the greatest respect and warmest friendship, I mention Miss Woodhouse; my father perhaps will think I ought to add, with deepest humility. A few words he uttered yesterday revealed his opinion, and some censure I acknowledge I am liable to. My conduct toward Miss Woodhouse, I believe, exceeded what was proper. To preserve a concealment so vital to me, I was led to employ more intimacy than was permissible in the situation we were thrust into. I cannot deny that Miss Woodhouse was my apparent object—but I am sure you will accept my declaration that, had I not been convinced of her indifference, I would not have been driven by any selfish motives to continue."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_028",
  "condition": "simplify",
  "rewritten_text": "I have already succeeded twice in asking for pardons, and now I may be too sure about yours and about any offense your friends might have committed. Please try to understand exactly why I was in such a difficult position when I first came to Randalls. I had a secret that I had to protect at all costs. That is the fact. Whether I was justified in putting myself in a situation that required such secrecy is another matter, and I will not argue it here. To explain why I felt it was my right, I point to a brick house in Highbury with windows set low on the ground floor and higher on the upper floor. I could not speak to her openly; the problems I faced at Enscombe were already well known and needed no further explanation. Fortunately, before we separated at Weymouth, I managed to win the most upright woman's heart to agree, in charity, to a secret engagement. Had she refused, I would have gone mad.\n\nYou may ask what I hoped to achieve by all this, what I was looking forward to. The answer is everything: time, chance, circumstance, slow developments, sudden bursts, perseverance, fatigue, health, and illness. Every possible good seemed within reach, and the first blessing was gaining her promise of faith and letters. If you need more detail, remember that I am your husband's son, and I have inherited a hopeful disposition that no property or land can match. Imagine me arriving on my first visit to Randalls under these conditions. I feel guilty because that visit could have happened earlier. You will see that I did not come until Miss Fairfax was in Highbury, and since you were the one slighted, I hope you will forgive me at once. I must also appeal to my father's compassion, reminding him that while I was away from his house I lost the blessing of knowing you. During the very happy fortnight I spent with you, I hope my conduct did not give you cause for criticism, except perhaps in one area.\n\nNow I come to the most important part of my behavior while I was with you, the part that makes me anxious and that needs a careful explanation. With the deepest respect and friendship, I must mention Miss Woodhouse. My father might think I should add an apology for any humiliation I caused. Yesterday he said a few words that revealed his opinion, and I accept that I may be subject to some censure. I think my conduct toward Miss Woodhouse was more forward than it should have been. In order to protect the secret that was so vital to me, I allowed a level of intimacy that went beyond what was proper. I cannot deny that Miss Woodhouse was the apparent object of my attention, but I am sure you will believe me when I say that if I had not been convinced of her indifference, I would not have been driven by selfish motives to continue."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_PRIDE_AND_PREJUDICE_012",
  "condition": "modernize",
  "rewritten_text": "Elizabeth was ready to see him at his best; and she couldn't help imagining that by showing off the room's good proportions, its appearance, and its furnishings, he was directing his attention especially toward her, as if he wanted her to realize what she missed by refusing him. But even though everything looked tidy and comfortable, she couldn't give him any sigh of regret; instead she watched her friend with amazement, wondering how she could be so cheerful with such a companion. Whenever Mr. Collins said something that might reasonably embarrass his wife—which happened quite often—Elizabeth involuntarily glanced at Charlotte. Occasionally she could see a faint blush, but most of the time Charlotte seemed to ignore it. After sitting long enough to admire every piece of furniture in the room, from the sideboard to the fire screen, and after giving an account of their journey and everything that had happened in London, Mr. Collins invited them to take a walk in the garden, which was large and well designed, and which he tended himself. Working in his garden was one of his most respectable pleasures; and Elizabeth admired the confident manner in which Charlotte spoke about the health benefits of the exercise, and admitted that she encouraged it as much as possible. Here, leading the way along every path and crossing, barely giving them a chance to respond to the compliments he demanded, he pointed out every view with such detail that the beauty was almost lost. He could count the fields in every direction and tell how many trees were in the farthest clump. But of all the sights his garden, the countryside, or the kingdom could offer, none compared with the view of Rosings, visible through a gap in the trees that bordered the park almost directly opposite his house. It was a handsome, modern building, well placed on rising ground.\n\nFrom his garden, Mr. Collins would have taken them around his two meadows; but the ladies, lacking shoes suitable for the lingering frost, turned back; and while Sir William walked with him, Charlotte led her sister and friend around the house, clearly delighted to show it without her husband's assistance. It was rather small, but well built and convenient; everything was arranged with a neatness and consistency that Elizabeth credited entirely to Charlotte. When Mr. Collins faded from attention, a genuine feeling of comfort filled the place, and from Charlotte's obvious enjoyment, Elizabeth guessed that he was often forgotten.\n\nShe had already learned that Lady Catherine was still in the countryside. The topic came up again at dinner, and when Mr. Collins joined in he said, \"Yes, Miss Elizabeth, you will have the honor of seeing Lady Catherine de Bourgh this Sunday at church, and I need not tell you how delighted you will be with her. She is full of kindness and superiority, and I have no doubt you will receive some of her attention after the service. I can say without hesitation that she will include you and my sister Maria in every invitation she extends to us during your stay here.\""
}

{
  "passage_id": "POE_WORKS_VOLUME_1_003",
  "condition": "paraphrase",
  "rewritten_text": "After about four and a half hours the balloon was sufficiently inflated, so I fastened the carriage to it and loaded all my gear—making sure not to omit the condensing apparatus, a generous supply of water, and a large stock of pemmican, which packs a great deal of nourishment into a small volume. I also placed a pair of pigeons and a cat in the carriage. Dawn was nearly upon us, and I felt it was the proper moment to depart. I pretended to drop a lit cigar on the ground, and while stooping to retrieve it I secretly ignited the slow‑match whose tip, as I have mentioned, projected only a little beyond the lower rim of one of the smaller casks. The three duns observed nothing, and as soon as I leapt into the carriage I cut the single cord that tethered me to the earth, delighted to find that I rose swiftly, bearing with ease one hundred and seventy‑five pounds of lead ballast, and capable of taking even more.\n\n scarcely had I reached a height of fifty yards when a terrible, roaring hurricane of flame, smoke, sulphur, limbs, gravel, burning wood, and blazing metal surged after me in a most dreadful and tumultuous fashion. My heart sank, and I fell to the bottom of the carriage trembling with unmitigated terror. It became clear that I had greatly exceeded the limits of my experiment and that the worst of the shock was still to come. In an instant the blood rushed to my temples, and a concussion—one I shall never forget—burst through the night, seeming to tear the very firmament asunder. Later, when I could reflect, I saw that the ferocity of the explosion was due to my position directly above it, in the line of its greatest force. At that moment, however, I thought only of saving my life. The balloon first collapsed, then violently expanded, then spun round and round with dreadful speed, and finally, reeling like a drunkard, flung me with great force over the rim of the carriage. I was left hanging at a terrible height, head down and face outward, by a slender cord about three feet long that had slipped through a crack near the bottom of the wickerwork, and as I fell my left foot became providentially caught in it. No mind can form an adequate picture of the horror of my plight. I gasped convulsively for breath, a shudder like a fit of ague shaking every nerve and muscle, my eyes seemed to pop from their sockets, a horrible nausea overwhelmed me, and at last I fainted.\n\nHow long I remained in that state I cannot say; it must have been a considerable interval, for when I began to regain a sense of being, I found day breaking, the balloon soaring at a prodigious altitude over a boundless ocean, with not a hint of land to be seen anywhere within the vast horizon."
}

{
  "passage_id": "POE_WORKS_VOLUME_1_003",
  "condition": "modernize",
  "rewritten_text": "After about four and a half hours I found the balloon fully inflated. I attached the car, packed all my gear inside—making sure to include the condensing apparatus, a generous supply of water, and a large amount of provisions like pemmican, which packs a lot of nutrition into a small volume. I also placed a pair of pigeons and a cat in the car. It was nearly dawn, and I thought it was time to depart. I dropped a lit cigar on the ground, pretending it was an accident, and while bending to pick it up I secretly lit the slow match whose tip, as I mentioned before, barely stuck out beyond the lower rim of one of the smaller barrels. The three duns didn’t notice at all; I jumped into the car, cut the single cord that tethered me to the ground, and was pleased to see the balloon rise, easily lifting the 175 pounds of lead ballast I had on board, and it could have carried even more.\n\nBut barely fifty yards up, a terrifying, roaring hurricane of fire, smoke, sulfur, limbs, gravel, burning wood, and blazing metal surged after me. My heart sank and I fell to the bottom of the car, trembling with sheer terror. I realized I had completely overreached, and that the worst of the shock was still to come. In an instant, all the blood rushed to my temples, and a concussion—one I’ll never forget—burst through the night, as if tearing the sky apart. Later, when I could think clearly, I blamed the extreme violence of the explosion on my position directly above it, right in the line of its greatest force. At the moment, I only cared about staying alive. The balloon first collapsed, then expanded violently, spun around at a frightening speed, and finally, staggering like a drunk, hurled me over the rim of the car. I was left hanging at a terrible height, head down and face outward, by a thin cord about three feet long that had slipped through a crack near the bottom of the wickerwork, and as I fell my left foot got caught in it. It’s impossible to fully convey the horror of that situation. I gasped for breath, my whole body shaking as if with a fever, my eyes felt like they were popping out, a horrible nausea overwhelmed me, and eventually I fainted.\n\nI can’t say how long I stayed unconscious. It must have been a considerable time, because when I finally regained some awareness, day was breaking. The balloon was at an enormous height over a vast ocean, and there was no land in sight anywhere on the endless horizon."
}

{
  "passage_id": "POE_WORKS_VOLUME_1_003",
  "condition": "simplify",
  "rewritten_text": "In about four and a half hours I had the balloon fully inflated. I attached the carriage, packed all my gear—especially the condensing apparatus, a large amount of water, and plenty of pemmican, which gives a lot of nutrition in a small package. I also put a pair of pigeons and a cat in the carriage. It was almost daybreak, and I thought it was time to go. I pretended to drop a lit cigar on the ground, and while bending to pick it up I secretly lit a slow‑match that stuck out just a little beyond the lower rim of one of the smaller barrels. The three duns didn’t notice anything. I jumped into the carriage, cut the single cord that held me to the ground, and was pleased to rise easily, carrying 175 pounds of lead ballast and able to lift even more.\n\nI had barely reached fifty yards in height when a massive, roaring hurricane of fire, smoke, sulphur, limbs, gravel, burning wood, and hot metal surged after me. My heart sank and I fell to the bottom of the carriage, trembling with pure terror. I realized I had gone too far and that the worst was still to come. In an instant all the blood rushed to my temples, and a concussion—one I will never forget—burst through the night like a crack in the sky. Later, when I could think clearly, I understood that the explosion’s extreme force was aimed right at me, directly in its line of greatest power. At the moment, I only thought of staying alive. The balloon first collapsed, then expanded violently, spun around at a frightening speed, and finally, like a drunk man stumbling, threw me over the rim of the carriage. I was left hanging at a great height, head down and face outward, by a thin cord about three feet long that had slipped through a crack near the bottom of the wicker. As I fell, my left foot got caught in the cord, which was a small mercy. It is impossible to describe fully the horror of that situation. I gasped for breath, my whole body shook as if I had a fever, my eyes felt like they were leaving their sockets, a terrible nausea overwhelmed me, and eventually I fainted.\n\nI cannot say how long I stayed unconscious. It must have been a considerable time, because when I began to regain awareness I saw the day breaking. The balloon was at an enormous height over a vast ocean, and there was no land in sight anywhere on the horizon."
}

