, shortly before I sealed the chamber’s mouth—the mercury reached its limit, or fell, in the barometer, which, as I have noted, was of an extended design. It then indicated an altitude of 132,000 feet, or twenty‑five miles, and at that moment I could survey an area of the earth amounting to no less than three‑hundred‑and‑twentieth of its total surface. At nine o’clock I again lost sight of land to the east, but not before noticing that the balloon was rapidly drifting toward the north‑north‑west. The convex curve of the ocean beneath me was very evident, though my view was often broken by the masses of cloud that floated to and fro."
