  "rewritten_text": "Next Sunday we all rode out to church, about three miles away, each on horseback. The men took their rifles with them, as did Buck, and kept them either between their knees or propped against the wall. The Shepherdsons did the same. The sermon was a stubborn sort—full of talk about brotherly love and the like, tiresome in its own way—but everyone said it was a good sermon. On the way home they all discussed it at length, talking about faith, good deeds, free grace, predestination, and all that sort of thing, and it struck me as one of the roughest Sundays I’d ever experienced.\n\nAbout an hour after dinner, everyone was dozing—some in chairs, some in their rooms—and the place grew pretty quiet. Buck and a dog were stretched out on the grass in the sun, sound asleep. I went up to our room and decided to take a nap myself. I found sweet Miss Sophia standing in the doorway next to ours; she slipped me into her room, closed the door gently, and asked if I liked her. I said I did. She then asked me to do something for her without telling anyone, and I agreed. She said she had left her Testament in a seat at church, between two other books, and wanted me to sneak out, fetch it, and keep it secret. I said I’d do it. So I slipped out and walked up the road; there was nobody at the church except maybe a hog or two, since the door wasn’t locked, and hogs like a cool floor in summer. Most folks only go to church when they have to, but a hog is a different matter.\n\nI thought to myself that something was odd; it wasn’t natural for a girl to be so nervous about a Testament. I gave the book a shake, and a small slip of paper fell out with “Half‑past two” penciled on it. I rifled through the book but found nothing else. I couldn’t make sense of it, so I slipped the paper back into the book. When I got home and went upstairs, Miss Sophia was waiting in her doorway. She pulled me in, shut the door, and began leafing through the Testament until she found the paper. As soon as she read it, she smiled, and before I could think, she grabbed me, gave me a squeeze, and told me I was the best boy in the world and to keep it quiet. Her face flushed red for a moment, her eyes lit up, and she looked strikingly pretty. I was quite astonished, but after catching my breath I asked what the paper meant. She asked if I had read it; I said no. She asked if I could read writing, and I replied, “No, only coarse‑hand.” She then explained that the paper was nothing more than a bookmark to hold her place, and that I could go play now."
}

