rry says, is to escape life’s suffering. I know you are surprised to hear me speak this way. You have not realized how I have grown. I was a schoolboy when you first knew me; I am a man now. I have new passions, new thoughts, new ideas. I am different, but you must not like me less. I have changed, yet you must always remain my friend. Of course I am very fond of Harry, but I know you are superior to him. You are not stronger—you are too fearful of life—but you are better. And how happy we once were together! Do not leave me, Basil, and do not quarrel with me. I am what I am. Nothing more needs to be said.\n\nThe painter felt an odd stirring within him. The young man was infinitely dear to him, and his character had become the great turning point in his art."
