scard them with a curious indifference that does not conflict with a genuine ardour of temperament, a condition, as some modern psychologists claim, that often accompanies true passion.\n\nIt was once rumored that he intended to join the Roman Catholic communion, and the Roman rite had always held a great attraction for him. The daily sacrifice, more dreadful indeed than all the ancient world’s offerings, moved him as much by its superb denial of sensory evidence as by the primitive simplicity of its elements and the eternal pathos of the human tragedy it sought to symbolize. He loved to kneel on the cold marble floor and watch the priest, in his stiff, flower‑embroidered dalmatic, slowly and with white hands lift the veil of the tabernacle, or raise the jeweled, lantern‑shaped monstrance holding that pallid wafer which one might be tempted to think is truly the \"panis cælestis,\" the bread of angels, or, robed in the garments of Christ’s Passion, break the Host into the chalice and strike his breast for his sins."
