  "rewritten_text": "Even reading about the splendor of the dead was a marvel.\n\nThen his curiosity turned to the embroidered hangings and tapestries that served as frescoes in the cold chambers of northern Europe. While he pursued the topic—he possessed an uncanny ability to become wholly engrossed in whatever occupied his mind—he felt a faint sorrow at the thought of how time ruins beautiful and wondrous things. He, however, seemed immune. Summer succeeded summer, the golden jonquils blossomed and withered countless times, and nights of terror replayed their tale of disgrace, yet he remained unchanged. No winter scarred his visage or dulled his flower‑like bloom. How unlike material objects! Where had they vanished to? Where was the great crocus‑hued robe, on which the gods battled the giants, once woven by brown maidens for Athena’s delight? Where the massive velarium that Nero stretched over the Roman Colosseum, that purple titan sail depicting the star‑studded sky and Apollo driving a chariot drawn by white, gilt‑reined horses? He yearned to see the curious table‑napkins fashioned for the Sun‑Priest, upon which lay every delicacy imaginable for a feast; the funeral cloth of King Chilperic, studded with three hundred golden bees; the fantastical robes that had provoked the Bishop of Pontus’s outrage, embroidered with “lions, panthers, bears, dogs, forests, rocks, hunters—indeed everything a painter might copy from nature”; and the coat once worn by Charles of Orleans, whose sleeves bore the verses of a song beginning “_Madame, je suis tout joyeux_,” the musical accompaniment rendered in gold thread, each square‑shaped note set with four pearls. He read of the chamber prepared at the palace of Rheims for Queen Joan of Burgundy, decorated with “thirteen hundred and twenty‑one parrots, embroidered and emblazoned with the king’s arms, and five hundred and sixty‑one butterflies, whose wings bore the queen’s arms, all worked in gold.” Catherine de’ Medici possessed a mourning bed of black velvet dusted with crescents and suns; its curtains were damask, festooned with leafy wreaths and garlands on a gold‑and‑silver ground, edged with pearl embroidery, standing in a room hung with rows of the queen’s devices in cut black velvet upon silver cloth. Louis XIV displayed gold‑embroidered caryatides fifteen feet high in his apartments. The state bed of Sobieski, King of Poland, was made of Smyrna gold brocade embroidered in turquoise with verses from the Koran; its supports were silver‑gilt, exquisitely chased and lavishly set with enamelled, jeweled medallions. It had been taken from the Turkish camp before Vienna, and Mohammed’s standard had stood beneath the trembling gilt canopy.\n\nThus, for an entire year, he endeavored to collect the most exquisite specimens of textile and embroidery he could locate: delicate Delhi muslins, finely worked with gold‑thread palmettes and over‑stitched with iridescent beetle wings; Dacca gauzes, whose transparency in the East is praised as “woven air,” “running water,” and “evening dew”; strange patterned cloths from Java; elaborate yellow Chinese hangings; books bound in tawny satin or pale blue silk, adorned with fleurs‑de‑lis, birds, and images; veils of lacis crafted in Hungarian point; Sicilian brocades and stiff Spanish velvets; Georgian pieces set with gilt coins; and Japanese foukousas, with their green‑tinged golds and marvelously feathered birds."
