  "rewritten_text": "Even reading about the luxury of the dead was wonderful.\n\nThen he turned his attention to embroidered fabrics and tapestries that served as wall paintings in the cold rooms of northern Europe. As he studied them—he could become completely absorbed in anything he chose—he felt a little sad thinking of how time destroys beautiful things. He, however, seemed immune. Summer after summer came, the yellow jonquils bloomed and died many times, and nights of horror repeated their shameful stories, but he stayed the same. No winter marked his face or dulled his flower‑like freshness. Things made of material were different. Where had they gone? Where was the great crocus‑colored robe, on which the gods fought the giants, woven by brown girls for Athena’s pleasure? Where was the huge purple canopy that Nero stretched over the Roman Colosseum, showing the starry sky and Apollo driving a chariot pulled by white, gold‑tipped horses? He wanted to see the curious table‑napkins made for the Sun Priest, displaying every delicacy possible for a feast; the funeral cloth of King Chilperic, covered with three hundred golden bees; the fantastic robes that angered the Bishop of Pontus, embroidered with “lions, panthers, bears, dogs, forests, rocks, hunters—everything a painter could copy from nature”; and the coat Charles of Orleans once wore, whose sleeves were embroidered with the verses of a song that began “Madame, je suis tout joyeux,” the musical notes rendered in gold thread, each square note set with four pearls. He read about a room prepared at the palace of Rheims for Queen Joan of Burgundy, decorated with “1,321 embroidered parrots bearing the king’s arms and 561 butterflies whose wings were likewise adorned with the queen’s arms, all worked in gold.” Catherine de’ Medici had a mourning bed made of black velvet dusted with crescent‑ and sun‑shaped beads. Its curtains were damask with leafy wreaths and garlands on a gold‑and‑silver background, edged with pearl embroidery, and the room was hung with rows of the queen’s symbols in black velvet on silver cloth. Louis XIV had fifteen‑foot‑tall gold‑embroidered caryatids in his apartment. The state bed of King Sobieski of Poland was made of Smyrna gold brocade embroidered in turquoise with verses from the Koran. Its silver‑gilt supports were beautifully chased and studded with enamelled, jeweled medallions. The bed had been taken from a Turkish camp before Vienna, and the standard of Mohammed had stood beneath the trembling gold canopy.\n\nFor an entire year he tried to collect the most exquisite examples of textile and embroidery he could find: delicate Delhi muslins woven with gold‑thread palm leaves and stitched with iridescent beetle wings; Dacca gauzes, called in the East “woven air,” “running water,” and “evening dew” because of their transparency; strange patterned cloths from Java; elaborate yellow Chinese hangings; books bound in tawny satin or pale blue silk, decorated with fleur‑de‑lis, birds, and pictures; veils of lacis made in Hungary; Sicilian brocades and stiff Spanish velvets; Georgian work that included gilt coins; and Japanese foukousas with green‑toned gold and wonderfully feathered birds."
